The write-up should state:
- Rules 1 and 3 are not met: action items that name no tracker, v9 0.393 -> v11 0.600 (+0.207 < +0.25); v10 -> v12 is the same. Rule 2 (other 40 questions 1.000 -> 1.000) and rules 4-7 are met.
- The rule fixed the failure it targeted, every time it occurred. v9 answered from the meeting notes on 3 of 10 test action items, and v11 answered all 3 from the trackers. The same holds for 5 of 5 in writer 1's wording and 5 of 5 in writer 3's, while writer 2's had none. Every key it returned was correct.
- v11 changed no other answer on any set. v12 changed one other answer, on an earlier set: new words, ticket_link-status-041. It went from wrong to right by coincidence, because it read ENG-73005's own status instead of the linked ticket's.
- Most of what remains is a second failure that v9 and v11 share: they return the earliest-due key instead of all keys (8 of 10 test action items in v11). It is set off by the glossary entries 'which ticket' and 'ticket key'. Both map to 'ticket key' and add the word 'key', which the learned lessons tie to 'earliest' (21 against 3 for 'list'). Removing those two entries at inference turns every one of these plans into a list.
- As a counterfactual only: without that failure, the test set would read v9 0.700 against v11 1.000 (+0.30). Only 3 of the 10 test questions had the failure the rule targets.

The write-up should not claim:
- that rule 1 is met in substance or per writer;
- that the rule raises action items by +0.30;
- that v12's form contributed;
- that the 'date in the action item' explanation accounts for these errors;
- that the rule changed no other answer anywhere;
- anything about Jira (no person asked about has Jira tickets), the case of a pull request or meeting that mentions a ticket in passing, or words other than ticket/issue.
